High level 
indexes are special data structures
store small portion of data
ordered and easy to search efficiently

"point to the document identity"

-support equality matches, range-based operations, and return sorted results
-ordered based on index fields and values when the index is created 

without indexes
has to do full scan
sort results in memory

by default, only one index created per collection, which includes only the _id field
every query should use an index

tradeoffs

pro:
speed up query performance
reduce disk I/O
reduce resources required

con:
write performance degradation from index
writes can significantly degrade if there are too many indexes
if insert new or update documents, index data structure needs to be updated as well
indexes need to be maintained and cleaned up if not used. or could degrade writes


index types

most common:
single field
compound field - more than one field in index
multikey index - operate on an array field

syntax:

db.collection.createIndex({birthdate:1})
db.collection.createIndex({birthdate:1}, {unique:1})

db.collection.getIndexes()

-can also view indexes in Atlas UI


createIndexes()	Create multiple indexes in one operation
getIndexes()	Return the collection’s index definitions
listIndexes()	Return an index cursor for inspection
indexExists()	Check whether named indexes exist
dropIndex()	Remove one specific index
dropIndexes()	Remove all indexes except the default _id index
hideIndex()	Hide an index from the query planner without deleting it
unhideIndex()	Make a hidden index visible again

Using explain() to look at what the winning plan is for the query.

Important fields include:

winningPlan — Plan MongoDB selected.
IXSCAN — Index scan; usually indicates an index is being used.
COLLSCAN — Collection scan; MongoDB examines documents directly.
nReturned — Documents returned.
totalKeysExamined — Index entries examined.
totalDocsExamined — Documents examined.
executionTimeMillis — Execution time.


Compound index:

db.customers.createIndex({
  active:1, 
  birthdate:-1,
  name:1
})

Order matters for compound index
Equalty, sort, range

Deletion:
all indexes can be deleted except default _id index

It's recommended to hide index first and then delete for production clusters

